$DIR/main.p:18:10: the memory allocated at main.p:9 is read here while the task started at main.p:13 may write it
    The task started at main.p:13 frees the memory allocated at main.p:9 at
    pluto/alloc/alloc.p:102, in Free called at main.p:15, and s waits for it at
    main.p:19. Meanwhile the memory allocated at main.p:9 is read at main.p:18
    in main, so the two may run at the same time, one of them writing: a data
    race.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Wait for the tasks before using the memory allocated at main.p:9:
        s.Wait()
    Or lock one sync.Mutex around every use of it, in the tasks and here:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)
